--[[
Whether a function that raises on purpose said so.

Raising is part of how a function is called. A caller who does not know has no
reason to be ready for it, and Lua has no signature to find it out from, so the
`---` run is the only place it can be said. `@raises` is where it is said, and
this asks a documented function that calls `error` to write one.

Three rules decide what is judged, and each of them is a choice:

Only a documented function. One with no `---` run has promised nothing, and a
lint that asked every function in a gradually typed language for a docblock
would be a different lint under a different name.

`error` counts and `assert` does not. There is no reason to write `error` except
to raise. Lua writes `assert` both for a caller who passed the wrong thing and
for an invariant its author believes cannot fail, and the call does not say
which; reading the second as a documented raise would ask for a promise about
something that never happens. Measured on this compiler, that spelling was a
caller's business one time in three, and a lint wrong that often is one a
project learns to silence.

A nested function's raises are its own, so the walk stops where a new body
begins. That is also what excuses a function handing a raising body to `pcall`.

It does not propagate through calls. Documenting what a callee raises is a claim
this checker cannot verify, and enforcing an unverified claim at every
intermediate frame is the trade that made `throws Exception` ubiquitous. A
caller gets the same fact by retrieval instead: `nupp lsp inspect` on the call
returns the callee's `@raises`. See `docs/lints.md`.
]]

local cst = require("nupp.cst")
local docblock = require("nupp.docblock")
local state = require("nupp.check.state")

local undocumentedraise = {}

--- What the walk calls into this judgement with: one call per named function body, at
--- the three places one is declared with a `---` run to read.
type undocumentedraise.Ops = {
    --- Reports when a documented function raises and its documentation does not say so.
    check: function(stat: any, body: any),}

-- Where this body raises on its own account, or nil if it does not. `error` is
-- recognized by name, the way `neverReturns` already recognizes it, so that untyped
-- code keeps the meaning it always had.
local function raisesAt(node: any): any
    if not node or cst.isToken(node) then
        return nil
    end
    local kind = node.kind
    if kind == "funcbody" or kind == "shortfn" then
        return nil
    end
    if kind == "call" then
        local callee = node.obj
        if callee and callee.kind == "name" and callee.token and callee.token.text == "error" then
            return callee.token
        end
    end
    for _, child in ipairs(node) do
        local at = raisesAt(child)
        if at then
            return at
        end
    end

    return nil
end

--- Installs the undocumented-raise judgement on one run.
---
--- @param c the run's state
--- @return the operations the walk calls
function undocumentedraise.install(c: state.Checker): undocumentedraise.Ops
    local ops = {}

    --- Reports `undocumented-raise` when a documented function raises and its run does
    --- not say so. The three places that call it are the three places a function body
    --- is declared with a name to document.
    ---
    --- @param stat the declaration, which is what carries the `---` run
    --- @param body its funcbody
    function ops.check(stat: any, body: any)
        if not stat or not body then
            return
        end
        local raiseTok = raisesAt(body.body)
        if not raiseTok then
            return
        end
        local info, documented = docblock.of(stat)
        if not documented or #info.raises > 0 then
            return
        end
        local label = stat.name and (cst.textOf(stat.name):gsub("%s+", "")) or "this function"
        -- The name it answers to, not the table it hangs off: `function query.Q:get` is
        -- reported at `get`, the way every other diagnostic about a member is.
        local at = stat.name and cst.lastToken(stat.name) or stat
        c.diag(
            "NUPP2506",
            at,
            ("%s raises, but its documentation does not say when"):format(label),
            nil,
            {help = "add an @raises line saying what makes it raise", related = {c.related(raiseTok, "raises here")}}
        )
    end

    return ops
end

return undocumentedraise
